Given this HTML and CSS, what will be the final color of the paragraph text? Explain step by step.

```html
<p id="intro" class="note important">Welcome to the fest!</p>
```

```css
p { color: black; }
.note { color: blue; }
#intro { color: green; }
.important { color: red; }
```

A student writes this CSS to style a button on hover and focus:
```css
button:hover { background-color: #007bff; color: white; }
button:focus { outline: N/A; }
```
Evaluate this code from an **accessibility** perspective. Is it good practice? Justify your answer.

questions.a:
Correct answer:
```css
#registration input[type="text"],
#registration input[type="email"],
#registration input[type="password"] {
  border: 1px solid gray;
  padding: 8px;
  font-size: 16px;
}

#registration input[type="text"]:focus,
#registration input[type="email"]:focus,
#registration input[type="password"]:focus {
  background-color: #ffffcc; /* light yellow */
}
```
Criteria for a good answer:  
- Uses attribute selectors `input[type="..."]` to target specific types (not all inputs).  
- Groups selectors with commas to avoid repetition.  
- Scopes to `#registration` form (ID selector).  
- Separates base styles from `:focus` state.  
- Uses valid hex/color name for light yellow.  
A poor answer uses `input { ... }` (styles checkboxes, radios, submit buttons too), misses `:focus`, or writes separate rule for each type without grouping — shows lack of selector efficiency and grouping knowledge.
